Section: The Bound Card

 Cells 36264 through 36319 of the low-memory band documented in
 ArchitectsSketchbook. The seam takes no device argument and there is
 nowhere else to keep one: a module-level record binding is a recipe
 rather than a cell, and referencing it allocates the record again on
 every reference, which on a path polled tens of millions of times per
 accept is the leak the control block exists to avoid.

 Every value here fits in 32 bits. The heap arena ends at 3 GB and a
 device BAR is refused unless it lands between 3 and 4 GB, so no address
 the seam stores can exceed the low word.

  net-driver-cb : Integer = 36264

  net-driver-off-card : Integer = 0
  net-driver-off-mmio : Integer = 8
  net-driver-off-rx-ring : Integer = 16
  net-driver-off-rx-bufs : Integer = 24
  net-driver-off-ctrl-blk : Integer = 32
  net-driver-off-tx-ring : Integer = 40
  net-driver-off-tx-bufs : Integer = 48

  net-card-ne2k : Integer = 0
  net-card-e1000 : Integer = 1

  net-driver-cell : Integer -> Integer
  net-driver-cell (off) = bit-and (peek-32 (net-driver-cb + off) 0) #FFFFFFFF

  net-driver-active : Integer
  net-driver-active = net-driver-cell net-driver-off-card

Section: Bind

  net-driver-bind-e1000 : E1000Device -> Integer
  net-driver-bind-e1000 (d) =
   if (d.e-present) == False then net-card-ne2k
   else if (d.e-mac-valid) == False then net-card-ne2k
   else let w1 = poke-32 (net-driver-cb + net-driver-off-mmio) 0 (d.e-mmio)
   in let w2 = poke-32 (net-driver-cb + net-driver-off-rx-ring) 0 (d.e-rx-ring)
   in let w3 = poke-32 (net-driver-cb + net-driver-off-rx-bufs) 0 (d.e-rx-bufs)
   in let w4 = poke-32 (net-driver-cb + net-driver-off-ctrl-blk) 0 (d.e-ctrl-blk)
   in let w5 = poke-32 (net-driver-cb + net-driver-off-tx-ring) 0 (d.e-tx-ring)
   in let w6 = poke-32 (net-driver-cb + net-driver-off-tx-bufs) 0 (d.e-tx-bufs)
   in let sel = poke-32 (net-driver-cb + net-driver-off-card) 0 net-card-e1000
   in net-card-e1000

Section: Bring-up

 The SHAPE here is card-independent and the finder behind it is not. If
 the sitting reports a part that is not Intel, `e1000-find` and
 `e1000-init` are what change and this function does not. That is the
 reason it holds no device knowledge of its own: no device-ID table, no
 quirk, no register.

  net-driver-bring-up : [Network.Read] Integer
  net-driver-bring-up =
   let card = when e1000-find pci-scan-all
     is Just (d) -> net-driver-bind-e1000 (e1000-init d)
     is None -> net-card-ne2k
   in act
    p <- net-driver-calibrate
    let w = poke-32 net-driver-poll-cell 0 p
    in card
   end

Section: The Poll Clock

 NetIO counts empty receive polls where another stack would read a clock,
 and the cost of one poll is a property of the DRIVER rather than of NetIO.
 An NE2000 poll is a port IN and therefore a VM exit; an e1000 poll reads a
 descriptor the card already wrote into RAM. Measured 2026-08-14 under
 codex-vm, one million empty polls: 15.52 s on the NE2000, 0.029 s on the
 e1000. 540 to one, and the real I219-V is a RAM read like the e1000.

 Every bound in NetworkStack is denominated in ticks, and the ratio between
 a measured round trip and a retransmit interval does hold at any steady
 rate, which is what that chapter's own argument rests on. What does not
 hold is anything pinned at a FIXED count of ticks: the initial RTO before
 a sample exists, the floor, the 48-tick clamp and the 141-tick give-up are
 wall-clock quantities in tick clothing. At 100000 polls a tick the e1000
 retransmits a SYN after 8.6 ms and abandons a live peer after 405 ms.

 So the interval is measured here, once, against the only clock this quire
 has. A guest that never calls net-driver-bring-up never pays for it and
 reads the fallback, which is the constant NetIO shipped: every plug keeps
 the numbers it was tuned with.

  net-driver-poll-cell : Integer = 36328

  net-driver-tick-ms : Integer = 100
  net-driver-poll-fallback : Integer = 100000
  net-driver-poll-floor : Integer = 1000
  net-driver-cal-batch : Integer = 1000
  net-driver-cal-fuel : Integer = 200000

 The clock is read once per BATCH and not once per poll. hpet-ticks is an
 MMIO read, which on the NE2000 costs about what the poll costs and on the
 e1000 costs hundreds of times more, so a per-poll clock check would
 measure the clock rather than the card and would answer nearly the same
 number for both.

  net-driver-poll-batch : Integer -> [Network.Read] Integer
  net-driver-poll-batch (n) =
   if n <= 0 then 0
   else let hp = __heap-save
   in act
    f <- net-driver-recv-frame
    let r = __heap-restore hp
    in net-driver-poll-batch (n - 1)
   end

  net-driver-cal-loop : Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Network.Read] Integer
  net-driver-cal-loop (deadline) (n) (fuel) =
   if fuel <= 0 then n
   else if hpet-ticks >= deadline then n
   else act
    z <- net-driver-poll-batch net-driver-cal-batch
    net-driver-cal-loop deadline (n + net-driver-cal-batch) (fuel - 1)
   end

 Calibration runs before any session exists, so a frame it polls up and
 drops is a frame that had no owner. Doing this lazily on the first connect
 or accept would not have that property: there the frame it dropped could
 be the SYN.

  net-driver-calibrate : [Network.Read] Integer
  net-driver-calibrate =
   let started = hpet-start
   in let rate = hpet-ticks-per-second
   in if rate <= 0 then net-driver-poll-fallback
   else let deadline = hpet-ticks + (rate * net-driver-tick-ms) / 1000
   in act
    n <- net-driver-cal-loop deadline 0 net-driver-cal-fuel
    if n < net-driver-poll-floor then net-driver-poll-floor else n
   end

 THE CELL IS CLAMPED AT BOTH ENDS, and the ceiling is derived rather than
 chosen. `net-driver-cal-loop` adds `net-driver-cal-batch` per iteration and
 runs at most `net-driver-cal-fuel` iterations, so the largest number
 calibration can possibly write is their product. A value above it did not
 come from calibration: the cell is 32 bits of memory read by `peek-32`, and
 nothing guarantees a bring-up ran, that this bring-up wrote it, or that the
 boot before this one left anything sane there.

 Answering the fallback rather than the ceiling is the point. A value larger
 than calibration can produce is not a measurement at all, and clamping it to
 the maximum legitimate measurement would report garbage AS a measurement --
 a plausible number in place of an honest one, which is the failure this tree
 has paid for twice this month. The low side has answered the fallback since
 it was written and this is the same answer at the other end.

 WHAT IT WAS COSTING (root, 2026-08-21): `net-io-max-polls` is this value
 times `net-io-max-ticks`, so an unclamped read multiplies straight into the
 ceiling of every NetIO wait. A stale cell of three billion makes that
 ceiling 1.5 trillion polls, and `net-io-send-drain` is entered once per
 CHUNK, so a stalled peer pays it per 1400 bytes rather than once per send.

  net-driver-cal-max : Integer = net-driver-cal-fuel * net-driver-cal-batch

  net-driver-poll-interval : Integer
  net-driver-poll-interval =
   let v = bit-and (peek-32 net-driver-poll-cell 0) #FFFFFFFF
   in if v < net-driver-poll-floor then net-driver-poll-fallback
   else if v > net-driver-cal-max then net-driver-poll-fallback
   else v

  net-driver-card-name : Integer -> Text
  net-driver-card-name (c) = if c == net-card-e1000 then "e1000" else "ne2000"

Section: Station Address

 It is read live off RAL/RAH rather than cached in the control block. A
 cached copy would be one more thing that can disagree with the hardware,
 and the two register reads cost less than the six-byte list they answer.

  net-driver-ne2k-mac : List Integer = [82, 84, 0, 18, 52, 86]

  net-driver-mac : List Integer
  net-driver-mac =
   if net-driver-active == net-card-e1000
   then e1000-read-mac (net-driver-cell net-driver-off-mmio)
   else net-driver-ne2k-mac

Section: Send

 e1000-send-at reads four addresses off an E1000Device and never reads
 the station address, so the record rebuilt here carries an empty MAC.
 It is rebuilt per FRAME SENT rather than per poll, which is the reason
 this side may spend an allocation the receive side may not.

  net-driver-e1000-device : E1000Device
  net-driver-e1000-device =
   E1000Device {
     e-mmio = net-driver-cell net-driver-off-mmio,
     e-rx-ring = net-driver-cell net-driver-off-rx-ring,
     e-tx-ring = net-driver-cell net-driver-off-tx-ring,
     e-rx-bufs = net-driver-cell net-driver-off-rx-bufs,
     e-tx-bufs = net-driver-cell net-driver-off-tx-bufs,
     e-ctrl-blk = net-driver-cell net-driver-off-ctrl-blk,
     e-present = True,
     e-mac = [],
     e-mac-valid = False,
     e-pch = 0, e-ulp = 0
   }

  net-driver-send-frame : List Integer -> [Network.Write] Integer
  net-driver-send-frame (frame) =
   if net-driver-active == net-card-e1000
   then e1000-send-frame net-driver-e1000-device frame
   else ne2k-send-frame frame

Section: Receive

 The receive path is polled up to fifty million times per accept, so it
 takes the raw entry point and builds no device record. The
 __heap-save / __heap-restore pair that makes those polls survivable
 stays in the callers, wrapped around this call.

  net-driver-recv-frame : [Network.Read] List Integer
  net-driver-recv-frame =
   if net-driver-active == net-card-e1000
   then (e1000-poll-raw
          (net-driver-cell net-driver-off-mmio)
          (net-driver-cell net-driver-off-rx-ring)
          (net-driver-cell net-driver-off-rx-bufs)
          (net-driver-cell net-driver-off-ctrl-blk)).r-frame
   else ne2k-recv-frame

